   "source": [
    "# CM3070 FieldServe CRM — Churn Prediction (UCI Online Retail II)\n",
    "**Model:** Binary classification — will a customer make no purchase in the next 180 days?  \n",
    "**Dataset:** UCI Online Retail II, reframed as field-service bookings  \n",
    "**Models compared:** Logistic Regression (RFM baseline) → LR (extended) → Random Forest → XGBoost  \n",
    "**Primary metric:** PR-AUC (imbalanced classes); also ROC-AUC, F1, Brier Score\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "## Why this dataset (not Olist)\n",
    "See report §3.x \"Dataset Justification: Churn Model Data Source Change.\" In short: Olist is a one-off marketplace where 97%+ of customers buy exactly once, making churn labels degenerate (near-constant majority class) even after restricting to repeat customers. Online Retail II has a much denser repeat-purchase population, which is what a churn model actually needs to learn from. **Trade-off:** this dataset has no customer-level coordinates, so `dist_from_centroid_km` is dropped here; the scheduling (`02_scheduling.ipynb`) and heat map (`03_heatmap.ipynb`) notebooks are unaffected and continue to use Olist.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "## How to get the data\n",
    "1. Go to https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mashlyn/online-retail-ii-uci (or https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/502/online+retail+ii)\n",
    "2. Download into `data/online_retail_ii/`\n",
    "3. The dataset is most commonly distributed as a single Excel file `online_retail_II.xlsx` with two sheets (`Year 2009-2010` and `Year 2010-2011`). Some Kaggle mirrors instead provide two CSVs. This notebook handles either — see the loader cell below.\n",
    "4. Columns expected: `Invoice` (or `InvoiceNo`), `StockCode`, `Description`, `Quantity`, `InvoiceDate`, `Price` (or `UnitPrice`), `Customer ID` (or `CustomerID`), `Country`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "de293c86",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Libraries loaded ✓\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n",
    "\n",
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── paths ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "DATA_DIR = Path('data/online_retail/')\n",
    "OUT_DIR  = Path('outputs')\n",
    "MODEL_DIR = Path('models')\n",
    "OUT_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
    "MODEL_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "RANDOM_STATE = 42\n",
    "np.random.seed(RANDOM_STATE)\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Libraries loaded ✓')"
   ]
  },
